import csv,json,hashlib
from pathlib import Path
ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
def tab(path):
    r=list(csv.reader(open(path))); h=r[0]
    out='| '+' | '.join(h)+' |\n|'+'---|'*len(h)+'\n'
    for row in r[1:]: out+='| '+' | '.join(row)+' |\n'
    return out
o=str(ROOT / 'analysis/out')+'/'
parts=[("# Supplementary file S5. Additional analyses for the present version\n\nTables S5.1–S5.8 and S5.10 are produced by `analysis/final_analysis.R` from the F5 commitment data and the report panels (outputs in `analysis/out/`). Table S5.9 is produced by `analysis/selection_flow.py`; Tables S5.11–S5.12 and their companion CSV files are produced by `analysis/build_selection_ledger.py`. Section S5.13 is generated by `analysis/build_first_report_supplement.py` from the pinned outputs of `analysis/analyse_first_followup.py` and `analysis/first_followup_focal_models.R` (hashes in `analysis/out/first_report_production_provenance.json`). Linear probability coefficients in percentage points; standard errors clustered by legal entity (commitments) or firm (reports); p-values unadjusted and exploratory.\n",None),
("## Table S5.1. Kazakhstan–Poland contrasts (coefficient on Kazakhstan, Poland reference)",'T_contrasts_KZ_PL.csv'),
("## Table S5.2. Leave-one-firm-out estimates, main specification\n\nFirm identifiers as stored in the replication data (Cyrillic for some Kazakhstani entities).",'T_lofo.csv'),
("## Table S5.3. Raw Kazakhstan–Poland difference and firm-level comparison\n\n10,000 bootstrap resamples of whole firms within countries (seed 20261015); firm means average commitments within entity.",'T_raw_bootstrap.csv'),
("## Table S5.4a. Outcomes by commitment year",'T_outcomes_year.csv'),
("## Table S5.4b. Outcomes by number of later reports",'T_outcomes_followup.csv'),
("## Table S5.5. Outcomes by state affiliation and country",'T_state_country.csv'),
("## Table S5.6. Specificity coefficient, leave-country-out",'T_specificity_lco.csv'),
("## Table S5.7. State affiliation and AI mentions in the report panel\n\nThe estimate for model `intensity` is in AI sentences per 100,000 characters.",'T_partA_state.csv'),
("## Table S5.8. Share of reports mentioning AI by country and year",'T_env_year.csv'),
("## Table S5.9. Selection flow by country and commitment year\n\nProduced by `selection_flow.py`; see `selection_flow_notes.md`. The 23 omissions between 213 and 190 have no archived individual reasons; the record-level ledger is reported below.",'T_selection_flow.csv'),
("## Table S5.10. Full confirmation by sector group and country",'T_sector_country.csv'),
("## Table S5.11. Reconstructed disposition of all 213 candidate keys",'T_selection_dispositions.csv')]
s=''
for h,f in parts: s+=h+'\n\n'+(tab(o+f)+'\n' if f else '')
s+='''
The reconstructed population contains 213 operational keys with available later reports: 33 Kazakhstani, 34 Polish and 146 Dutch. Of these, 190 entered the archived initial outcome coding. Six subsequently received a documented `not_a_promise` decision at F3 and one at F4, leaving 183 final records. For the other 23 keys (4 Kazakhstani, 4 Polish and 15 Dutch), the pre-coding omission is observable but its individual reason is unresolved. The historical code can omit both empty verdicts and `not_a_promise` verdicts; this does not identify which mechanism applied to any missing key. None of the 23 omitted keys has been re-adjudicated, restored or assigned an outcome for this ledger.

`selection_ledger_213.csv` is the complete machine-readable ledger. It records each four-part key and reconstructed promise ID, the first retained fragment and its archived excerpt, all candidate fragment IDs, later-report filenames and years, membership at each coding stage, disposition, recorded reason and evidence source. `selection_ledger_unresolved_23.csv` isolates the unknown cases. `selection_ledger_fragment_links.csv` provides one row per candidate-fragment link. `selection_ledger_audit.json` records checks, unresolved IDs and input hashes. All four files are in `analysis/out/` of the local preparation package; this statement does not assert that this version has been publicly released.

Promise IDs are reconstructed from the historical sorted order of the 213 keys. All 184 F3 records match the reconstructed country, firm, category and year, and all 183 final records also match their archived number of later reports. These checks establish consistency with archived analytical files, not independent verification of unique project identity, commitment eligibility or implementation. The retained quotations are retrieval excerpts, not newly verified complete source passages.

Follow-up availability is reconstructed from panel metadata and the historical four-report exclusion list. Historical script 21 checks text-file size in **bytes** (at least 50,000); the reconstruction uses the panel's **character** count (at least 50,000). Those filters are not identical. The count agreement for final records does not establish that all unavailable or excluded source texts have been recovered or re-extracted. No new full-text audit was performed.

## Table S5.12. Candidate-key register

Status codes: **R** = retained in final F5; **E3** = documented non-commitment exclusion at F3; **E4** = documented non-commitment exclusion at F4; **U** = omitted before initial coding, individual reason unknown. The full recorded reasons for E3/E4 and source references are in the companion ledger; blank reasons for U mean unknown, not a negative eligibility decision. A firm × use-category × year key is not a verified unique AI project.

'''
codes={'included_final':'R','excluded_non_commitment_F3':'E3',
       'excluded_non_commitment_F4':'E4','omitted_before_initial_coding_unresolved':'U'}
s+='| ID | Country | Firm identifier | Year | Use category | Candidate fragments | Later reports | Status |\n'
s+='|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n'
for row in csv.DictReader(open(o+'selection_ledger_213.csv',encoding='utf-8')):
    values=[row[k] for k in ['promise_id','country','firm','year','category','n_candidate_fragments','n_later_reports']]+[codes[row['disposition']]]
    s+='| '+' | '.join(x.replace('|','\\|') for x in values)+' |\n'
extension = ROOT / 'work/S5_first_report_extension.md'
if extension.exists():
    pin=json.loads((ROOT/'analysis/out/first_report_supplement_provenance.json').read_text())
    production=ROOT/'analysis/out/first_report_production_provenance.json'
    assert hashlib.sha256(extension.read_bytes()).hexdigest()==pin['extension_sha256']
    assert hashlib.sha256(production.read_bytes()).hexdigest()==pin['production_manifest_sha256']
    records=json.loads(production.read_text())
    for group in ['inputs','outputs']:
        for path,digest in records[group].items():assert hashlib.sha256((ROOT/path).read_bytes()).hexdigest()==digest, 'Stale first-report artifact: '+path
    # Keep the legacy ledger's audit scope distinct from the subsequent exercise.
    s = s.replace('No new full-text audit was performed.', 'No new full-text audit was performed for this ledger reconstruction; the separate first-report exercise is documented in Section S5.13 below.')
    lines = extension.read_text().splitlines()
    lines[0] = '## Section S5.13. Provisional first-available-report comparison'
    lines = [line if i == 0 or not line.startswith('## ') else '### '+line[3:] for i,line in enumerate(lines)]
    s += '\n\n'+'\n'.join(lines)+'\n'
(ROOT / 'work/S5.md').write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
